  <!-- Section 9: Common Mistakes -->
  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="section__container">
      <h2 class="section__title">Common Mistakes Brands Make with Leather Manufacturing</h2>
      <p class="section__text">
        Working with leather manufacturers presents unique challenges. Many brands make avoidable mistakes that impact quality, durability, and customer satisfaction. Learn from these common pitfalls.
      </p>
      
      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Compromising on Leather Quality</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Some brands choose lower-quality leather to reduce costs, resulting in garments that don't perform or last. Leather quality directly impacts garment durability and customer satisfaction. We advise on appropriate leather quality for your price point - better to reduce style count than compromise on leather quality.
      </p>
      
      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Ignoring Grain Orientation</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Leather has grain orientation that affects stretch and durability. Some manufacturers don't consider grain direction when cutting, resulting in garments that stretch unevenly or lose shape. We always cut considering grain orientation to maintain structural integrity and proper fit.
      </p>
      
      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Using Inappropriate Hardware</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Hardware quality and type significantly impact leather garment functionality. Some brands choose cheap hardware that fails or doesn't complement leather aesthetics. We source quality hardware - YKK zippers, solid metal buckles - that ensures durability and cohesive design.
      </p>
      
      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Neglecting Edge Finishing</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Leather edges require proper finishing - painting, burnishing, or binding - to prevent fraying and ensure clean appearance. Some manufacturers skip proper edge finishing, resulting in garments that deteriorate quickly. We always apply appropriate edge finishing for durability and aesthetics.
      </p>
      
      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Not Verifying Sustainability</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Leather sustainability is increasingly important to consumers. Some brands don't verify leather certification, risking sourcing from irresponsible tanneries. Always request LWG certification for leather sustainability claims. We provide complete LWG documentation for all leather sourcing.
      </p>
      
      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Insufficient Sample Testing</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Leather garments should be tested for wear and fit before bulk production. Some brands approve samples after only visual inspection, missing issues revealed through actual use. We recommend wear testing samples to verify leather performance, construction durability, and comfort before bulk orders.
      </p>
